From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:38:29 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: Implement wlr-screencopy-unstable-v1

Let clients such as grim, slurp + grim, wf-recorder, hyprpicker and
wayvnc copy the contents of an output, or of a region of it, into a
wl_shm buffer.

Frames are painted from the stage with clutter_stage_paint_to_buffer(),
like the screen cast area streams, into an XRGB8888 buffer that has the
orientation of the output's buffer (clients apply the wl_output
transform, as on wlroots); they are never y-inverted. The cursor is
included only with overlay_cursor. 'copy' completes on the next idle;
'copy_with_damage' completes once the captured area has been damaged
since the previous copy_with_damage of the same manager object on that
output (the first one at once, with full damage), and reports that
damage in buffer coordinates. The stage is only watched while a client
waits for damage or keeps a damage history. Frames fail when their
output goes away or the monitor configuration changes.

The protocol gives every client the screen contents without asking, so
it is gated by the new org.gnome.mutter.wayland allow-screen-capture
setting (on by default). When it is off the global is hidden from new
clients and all pending and new captures fail. Clients in a Flatpak
sandbox are never offered the global; they keep using the screen cast
portal. Each manager object logs once which process is capturing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
